[
    {
        "content": "<p>Do we have a theorem similar to the following in Mathlib?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Analysis</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Normed</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Basic</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Analysis</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Normed</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Operator</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">LinearIsometry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exists_isometry</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hxy</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃ₗᵢ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Probably more assumptions are need on <code>E</code>. When <code>E</code> is finite dimensional, we can choose <code>f</code> to be a rotation.</p>",
        "id": 584527280,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1775763534
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is not what you are asking for, but we have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=SeparatingDual.exists_continuousLinearEquiv_apply_eq#doc\">docs#SeparatingDual.exists_continuousLinearEquiv_apply_eq</a></p>",
        "id": 584544348,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1775770273
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think you probably need to assume <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=InnerProductSpace#doc\">docs#InnerProductSpace</a>, right? IIRC you cannot hope for this to happen in an <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> space for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p \\neq 2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace nobreak\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>.</p>",
        "id": 584544718,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1775770461
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes I did some literature review and apparently this is related to an open problem called <a href=\"https://mathoverflow.net/questions/41211/easy-proof-of-the-fact-that-isotropic-spaces-are-euclidean?_gl=1*rui9jh*_ga*MTgyNDE3MDk2Ni4xNzc1MjAzNjQ3*_ga_S812YQPLT2*czE3NzU3NzAzODUkbzEkZzEkdDE3NzU3NzAzOTQkajU1JGwwJGgw\">Banach-Mazur rotation problem</a>. But the proof in the case of InnerProductSpace should be relatively easy. Maybe I'll give it a try in the next few days.</p>",
        "id": 584545001,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1775770649
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I was remembering correctly : the Banach-Lamperti theorem puts strong restrictions on what isometries of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> look like for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\neq 2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace nobreak\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span> : they come from either measure-class-preserving automorphisms (modulo measure zero) of the space, or multiplication by a unit-circle-valued measurable function.</p>",
        "id": 584545320,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1775770821
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Apparently we do have this theorem...<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Analysis/InnerProductSpace/Projection/Reflection.html#Submodule.reflection_sub\">Submodule.reflection_sub</a></p>",
        "id": 584548829,
